\begin{abstract}
Vision token pruning reliably accelerates Vision Transformer (ViT) inference with negligible accuracy loss, prompting recent proposals to apply the same technique to Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models for faster robotic control. We conduct the first cross-architecture closed-loop evaluation of vision token reduction on two VLA architectures and find that compressibility is strongly associated with the vision connector design. SmolVLA, whose pixel-shuffle connector spatially folds patches into high-density tokens, exhibits a cliff-edge failure: all tested pruning configurations collapse from 44.8\% to 0\% success rate on LIBERO, regardless of scoring function, retention ratio, or mechanism. OpenVLA, whose MLP projector maps patches independently, degrades gracefully from 74.0\% to 37.4\% under random token removal. We trace the divergence to token information density: pixel-shuffle produces tokens with near-uniform attention across all transformer layers, while OpenVLA develops progressively selective attention in deeper layers. For architectures where pruning fails, a learned cross-attention module overcomes the cliff-edge by re-encoding tokens rather than deleting them, raising success rate to 61.6\% with 75\% fewer tokens, a performance-neutral deployment benefit. These results establish that VLA token compressibility is architecture-dependent and that cross-attention re-encoding can overcome failures that all deletion-based methods share.
\end{abstract}

